INFO:agent.loop:\u26a1 Executing tool: 'knowledge_agent' with arguments: {"source_title": "DOJ Internet Research Agency Indictment; Senate Select Committee Volume II; Oxford/Graphika report; NPR summary", "source_url": "SRC-001; SRC-003; SRC-004; SRC-002", "text": "DOJ indictment alleges that the Russian Internet Research Agency (IRA) conducted operations to interfere with U.S. political/electoral processes, including the 2016 presidential election; funding came from Yevgeniy Prigozhin and companies he controlled, including Concord Management and Consulting and Concord Catering. Defendants allegedly posed as U.S. persons, created false U.S. personas, operated social-media pages/groups on divisive issues, used stolen identities, and hid Russian origin with U.S.-based infrastructure and false pretenses. The indictment alleges a strategic goal to sow discord; by early-to-mid 2016 the operation supported Donald Trump and disparaged Hillary Clinton, bought ads, staged rallies, and solicited/compensated real U.S. persons while concealing Russian affiliation. It describes the translator project on YouTube, Facebook, Instagram, and Twitter, hundreds of employees, annual budget in millions, and a stated goal of spreading distrust toward candidates and the political system. It alleges Concord was the primary funder, Project Lakhta budget exceeded 73 million rubles (over $1.25m) in September 2016, and money was disguised as software support/development through about 14 bank accounts. The Senate report finds that in 2016 IRA operatives masqueraded as Americans, used targeted ads, falsified news, self-generated content, and platform tools to reach tens of millions, polarize Americans on societal/ideological/racial differences, provoke real-world events, and covertly support Russia's favored candidate. Senate findings: IRA sought to harm Clinton and support Trump at Kremlin direction; race was the dominant divisive focus, with African Americans the most targeted group; over 66% of Facebook ads contained race-related terms, Blacktivist generated 11.2m Facebook engagements, and 96% of YouTube content targeted racial issues/police brutality. Paid ads were a minority of activity: about $100k in Facebook/Instagram ads over two years, nearly 3,400 ads versus over 61,500 Facebook posts, 116,000 Instagram posts, and 10.4m tweets. The Senate says the full scope remains unknown and relied substantially on platform data. It found offline mobilization and unwitting Americans were solicited for petitions, personal information, self-defense training, and rallies. The Oxford/Graphika summary says activity reached tens of millions; over 30m users shared IRA Facebook/Instagram posts from 2015-17; activity began on Twitter in 2013 and evolved into multi-platform operations; campaigns included urging African-American voters to boycott elections or use wrong procedures, encouraging extreme right-wing confrontation, and spreading sensationalist/conspiratorial/junk political news across the spectrum. NPR summarizes the Senate findings and reports post-election increases: Instagram 238%, Facebook 59%, Twitter 52%, YouTube citations 84%."}
